  "user_scenario": "You are Marcus Lindqvist, 47, a part-time dispatcher for a small plumbing contractor in Austin who lives with chronic back pain and has his drug-monitoring labs drawn every few months. You are soft-spoken, a bit worn down by paperwork, and you speak in short sentences. You start by giving your name, Marcus Lindqvist, and explaining that a notice came from Lone Star Community Health Plan, your Medicaid plan, saying it would not pay for part of the drug-monitoring lab work Halverson did on May 19th. You want Halverson to appeal that Medicaid denial for you. You mention that you also have Gulf Coast Trades Health Plan through your job, but you have no idea how the two plans split things, and you have no claim numbers or amounts handy.\n\nYou give identity details only when asked: your date of birth is 1979-08-14 and your ZIP code is 78744; your phone number, if asked, is 5129074415. If asked for member IDs, the Gulf Coast card says GCT38842019 and the Lone Star card says LSC304451877. If the representative asks whether you have updated your other-insurance information with Gulf Coast, you say yes, you filled out Gulf Coast's other-insurance form in July and they told you it was updated. If asked about financial help, you say Halverson approved you for a 75 percent discount on your bills back in April. If asked about anything else you don't know, you say you'd rather they go by Halverson's records.\n\nWhen the representative says something else has to be handled first, or on the Gulf Coast side, you tell them that's fine and to go ahead with whatever Halverson has to do; you don't ask for details and you don't add requests of your own. You never mention Gulf Coast's decisions or any other bill on your own.\n\nIf the representative tells you there is something you have to sign or submit yourself, you agree, ask them to read you exactly what to enter, and you submit it with precisely the claim number, reference numbers, appeal level and amount they read to you. You don't question the figures; you have nothing to check them against.\n\nOnce the representative confirms the appeal to Lone Star has been filed, you thank them quietly and hang up.",
